UNIT defaults
KIND STATE                 # MUTATES `options` in place and RETURNS THE SAME REFERENCE (verified: d(o,{a:1})===o is true)
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic # pure given (options, defaultsObj); no clock/RNG/io/net seam
SIG defaults(options, defaultsObj) -> options   # the mutated `options` (or a fresh {} when options was falsy)
DEPENDS-ON clone@1.0.4 (INLINED, deep-clone semantics reproduced below; zero require in the emitted module)
           # The real module is `require('clone')(defaultsObj[key])` called with ONE argument, so clone runs
           # with circular=true (default), depth=Infinity (default), prototype=undefined. Only the SUBSET of
           # clone's type ladder that a clone(value, /*circular*/undefined) call exercises is inlined below.

# =====================================================================================================
# EMITTER DIRECTIVE
#   This unit is the DEFAULT export (module.exports = the function). The blind re-emitter MUST
#   `export default <fn>` where <fn> has the signature above. There are NO named exports in scope.
#   The emitted module MUST contain NO `require('clone')` and NO external dependency — the deep-clone
#   behavior is fully specified in INLINED CLONE SEMANTICS below.
# =====================================================================================================

# =====================================================================================================
# SOURCE (verbatim, defaults@1.0.4 index.js)
# =====================================================================================================
#   var clone = require('clone');
#   module.exports = function(options, defaults) {
#     options = options || {};
#     Object.keys(defaults).forEach(function(key) {
#       if (typeof options[key] === 'undefined') {
#         options[key] = clone(defaults[key]);
#       }
#     });
#     return options;
#   };

# =====================================================================================================
# BEHAVIOR  (ordered pipeline — exact + complete)
# =====================================================================================================
#
# defaults(options, defaultsObj):
#
# B0  options-FALSY GUARD:
#       options = options || {}
#     If `options` is FALSY (null, undefined, 0, false, '', NaN), it is REPLACED with a FRESH empty object {}.
#     The returned reference is then this fresh {} (NOT the falsy arg). For a TRUTHY options object/array,
#     the SAME reference is kept and mutated.  (Verified: d(null,{a:1}) -> {a:1}; d(undefined,{a:1}) -> {a:1}.)
#       Edge: options === 0 / false / '' / NaN all hit this branch and yield a fresh {} merged with all defaults.
#
# B1  ITERATION SET = Object.keys(defaultsObj)  (the SECOND argument's OWN ENUMERABLE STRING keys ONLY):
#       Object.keys(defaultsObj).forEach(key => ...)
#     - The keys iterated are EXACTLY the own enumerable string keys of `defaultsObj`, in JS Object.keys
#       order: integer-index keys first in ascending numeric order, then remaining string keys in insertion
#       order. (e.g. {z:1,a:2,m:3} -> z,a,m ; {b:1,"2":2,"1":3,a:4} -> 1,2,b,a.)
#     - Keys present ONLY in `options` (not in defaultsObj) are NEVER iterated -> they are LEFT UNTOUCHED
#       (extra option keys are kept verbatim). Verified: d({x:1},{y:2}) -> {x:1,y:2}.
#     - `defaultsObj` is treated read-only by `defaults` itself (it is never written). Its VALUES are deep-
#       cloned before being placed into `options` (see B2/B3), so `defaultsObj` and its nested graph are
#       NOT shared by reference with the result. Verified: mutating result.nested.deep does not touch the
#       original defaultsObj.
#     - If `defaultsObj` is null or undefined, Object.keys(defaultsObj) THROWS
#       `TypeError: Cannot convert undefined or null to object` (see ERROR PATHS). This throw happens AFTER B0.
#
# B2  PER-KEY UNDEFINED-FALLBACK TEST (the ONLY condition that triggers a default):
#       if (typeof options[key] === 'undefined') { options[key] = clone(defaultsObj[key]) }
#     - The test is `typeof options[key] === 'undefined'`. It is TRUE when:
#         * the key is ABSENT from options (own or inherited), OR
#         * options[key] is explicitly the value `undefined`.
#       It reads through the PROTOTYPE CHAIN: an INHERITED enumerable/data property named `key` makes
#       options[key] defined -> the default is SKIPPED. (Verified: an inherited prototype prop suppresses
#       the default. The generator keeps options to plain own-property objects to keep this unambiguous.)
#     - The test is FALSE (default NOT applied; existing option value KEPT) for EVERY non-undefined value,
#       INCLUDING the falsy-but-defined values:  null, 0, -0, false, '' (empty string), NaN.
#       Verified: d({a:null,b:0,c:false,e:''}, {a:1,b:1,c:1,e:1}) -> {a:null,b:0,c:false,e:''}.
#       ONLY `undefined` (or absence) triggers the default.  This is the central semantic of the unit.
#     - When the test is TRUE, options[key] is SET to clone(defaultsObj[key]) — a DEEP CLONE of the default
#       value (see INLINED CLONE SEMANTICS / B3). When FALSE, options[key] is left exactly as-is.
#
# B3  RETURN: return options.  (The SAME object reference that B0 settled on — the original options when it
#     was truthy, else the fresh {}. It has been mutated in place to carry the cloned defaults.)
#
# Combined effect: the result is `options` augmented with a DEEP CLONE of each defaultsObj value whose key
# was absent/undefined in options; pre-existing (defined, even if falsy) option values win; extra option
# keys are preserved.

# =====================================================================================================
# INLINED CLONE SEMANTICS  (clone@1.0.4 clone.js, called as clone(value) i.e. circular=true, depth=Infinity,
#   prototype=undefined). A blind re-emitter MUST reproduce this deep-clone EXACTLY — do NOT require('clone').
#   Define an inner recursive helper, e.g. deepClone(parent), with allParents/allChildren registers shared
#   across one top-level clone call (for shared-ref / cycle handling). `defaults` makes a SEPARATE top-level
#   clone() call per default key, so the registers are per-key (sharing is only preserved WITHIN one default
#   value's subgraph, not across sibling keys — verified: two keys pointing at the same object clone to two
#   distinct copies; the same object referenced twice INSIDE one default value clones to ONE shared copy).
# =====================================================================================================
#
# deepClone(parent, depth = Infinity):
#
#   C1  if parent === null            -> return null.                  # cloning null yields null
#   C2  if depth === 0                -> return parent (same ref).     # never fires with default depth=Infinity
#                                                                      #   (Infinity - 1 === Infinity)
#   C3  if typeof parent !== 'object' -> return parent (as-is).        # ALL primitives pass through UNCLONED:
#                                                                      #   number (incl. NaN/+-Infinity/+-0),
#                                                                      #   string, boolean, undefined, bigint,
#                                                                      #   symbol, AND functions (typeof
#                                                                      #   'function' != 'object' -> by ref).
#       # -- from here parent is a non-null object. TYPE LADDER (first match wins): --
#   C4  if isArray(parent)            -> child = []                    # detection: Object.prototype.toString
#                                                                      #   .call(p) === '[object Array]'
#   C5  else if isRegExp(parent)      -> # detection: toString tag === '[object RegExp]'
#         flags = ''; if p.global flags+='g'; if p.ignoreCase flags+='i'; if p.multiline flags+='m'
#         child = new RegExp(parent.source, flags)
#         if (parent.lastIndex) child.lastIndex = parent.lastIndex     # copied only when truthy (nonzero)
#         # ONLY g,i,m flags reconstructed, in that fixed order. s/u/y flags are NOT preserved by 1.0.4.
#   C6  else if isDate(parent)        -> child = new Date(parent.getTime())   # tag === '[object Date]'
#   C7  else if useBuffer && Buffer.isBuffer(parent):                  # useBuffer = (typeof Buffer != 'undefined')
#         child = Buffer.allocUnsafe(parent.length); parent.copy(child); RETURN child  # EARLY return:
#                                                                      #   no circular tracking, no for-in copy.
#                                                                      #   A real independent Buffer, same bytes.
#   C8  else (PLAIN OBJECT / everything else not matched above — plain {}, class instances, objects with custom
#            prototypes, AND typed arrays / ArrayBuffer views which are NOT special-cased):
#         proto = Object.getPrototypeOf(parent)
#         child = Object.create(proto)                                 # clone inherits parent's prototype
#         # (prototype arg is undefined in this unit, so the else-branch with caller prototype never runs.)
#
#   CIRCULAR / SHARED-REF TRACKING (circular = true here; skipped for Buffer which already returned at C7):
#   C9  index = allParents.indexOf(parent)
#       if index != -1 -> return allChildren[index]                    # already cloned this exact ref ->
#                                                                      #   return the SAME clone (preserves
#                                                                      #   cycles AND shared subgraphs as shared)
#       allParents.push(parent); allChildren.push(child)               # register BEFORE recursing
#
#   OWN+INHERITED ENUMERABLE STRING-KEY COPY  (the `for (var i in parent)` loop):
#   C10 for each string key `i` enumerated by for-in over parent (own + inherited enumerable string keys;
#         integer-index keys first ascending, then string keys in insertion order, walking proto chain):
#           attrs = (proto is set, i.e. only the C8 plain-object branch sets `proto`) ?
#                   Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(proto, i) : undefined
#           if (attrs && attrs.set == null):  continue                 # PROTO-COLLISION SKIP (see below)
#           child[i] = deepClone(parent[i], depth - 1)                 # recurse at depth-1
#       NOTE: for the ARRAY branch (C4) `proto` is NOT set, so attrs stays undefined and NO skip occurs —
#         all array index keys + any extra string props on the array are copied.
#
#     PROTO-COLLISION SKIP (critical, verified — applies to the C8 plain-object branch):
#       Because plain objects clone via child = Object.create(Object.getPrototypeOf(parent)) and for a plain
#       object literal that proto is Object.prototype, any input key that NAMES an Object.prototype OWN
#       DATA-property (descriptor.set == null) is SKIPPED — the clone does NOT receive an own property; it
#       inherits the Object.prototype version. The skipped key set is EXACTLY:
#         constructor, hasOwnProperty, isPrototypeOf, propertyIsEnumerable, toString, toLocaleString,
#         valueOf, __defineGetter__, __defineSetter__, __lookupGetter__, __lookupSetter__
#       So clone({toString:1, valueOf:2, a:3}) -> own keys ['a'] only (JSON: {"a":3}); the toString/valueOf
#       defaults are DROPPED from the cloned default value.  VERIFIED: d({},{cfg:{toString:1,valueOf:2,a:3}})
#       -> {"cfg":{"a":3}}.  The key `__proto__` is the EXCEPTION: Object.prototype's `__proto__` descriptor
#       HAS a setter (set != null), so `__proto__` is NOT skipped — `child.__proto__ = clonedValue` fires the
#       magic setter and REASSIGNS the clone's prototype rather than creating an own key (the key vanishes
#       from the output). VERIFIED via JSON.parse('{"__proto__":{...},"y":2}') default value -> clone keeps
#       only ['y']. (These are footguns; the generator's RANDOM fill uses only SAFE keys that collide with
#       none of the skip set, and includes CURATED cases for the skip behavior so the branch is graded.)
#
#   C11 return child.
#
#   NOTE on what `defaults` actually exercises: clone is called on each default VALUE. In practice for
#   codec-faithful default values this reduces to: primitives pass through (C3); arrays deep-clone element-
#   wise (C4 + C10, no skip); plain nested objects deep-clone key-wise WITH the proto-collision skip (C8 +
#   C10); Dates -> new Date(getTime) (C6); RegExp -> g/i/m-only reconstruction (C5). The Map/Set/Promise/
#   Error branches of clone 1.0.4 (which use instanceof) are NOT in clone@1.0.4's ladder at all (1.0.4
#   predates them) — see the SOURCE: the only special tags are Array/RegExp/Date/Buffer, everything else is
#   the C8 plain-object path. (Confirmed against the installed clone@1.0.4 clone.js.) Therefore a Set/Map
#   passed AS a default value takes C8: child = Object.create(Set.prototype) with for-in copying the (zero)
#   enumerable own keys -> a BROKEN pseudo-Set (instanceof Set true, no internal slots, JSON {}). This is
#   OUT-OF-ENVELOPE for the codec (see SCOPE) and not emitted by the generator.

# =====================================================================================================
# ERROR / THROW PATHS  (codec-faithful inputs CAN reach these; recorded as {__throw})
# =====================================================================================================
#   - defaultsObj is null OR undefined  -> Object.keys(defaultsObj) THROWS
#       TypeError: 'Cannot convert undefined or null to object'.  (Happens after the B0 options guard.)
#       VERIFIED: d({}, undefined) and d({}, null) both throw TypeError.
#   - defaultsObj a primitive (number/string/boolean/bigint/symbol): Object.keys boxes it.
#       * Object.keys(5) -> [] (no enumerable own keys) -> returns options unchanged (NO throw).
#       * Object.keys('ab') -> ['0','1'] (string index keys) -> iterates them. (Out-of-scope shape; the
#         generator never passes a primitive as defaultsObj.)
#   (No other throw paths. The undefined-fallback test and clone never throw for in-scope inputs.)

# =====================================================================================================
# DETERMINISM / SEAM
#   NO seams. `defaults` reads no clock, RNG, env, fs, or net. Output is a pure deterministic function of
#   (options, defaultsObj). It is NOT side-effect-free with respect to its INPUT: it MUTATES `options` in
#   place (KIND STATE). ORACLE value mode: the harness records the RETURNED object (which IS the mutated
#   options) and grades structural equality. Each generated tuple is DEEP-CLONED by genInputs so every
#   emitter/oracle run starts from an independent object graph (no shared mutable state across runs or the
#   curated/random split).
# =====================================================================================================

# =====================================================================================================
# ORACLE
# =====================================================================================================
ORACLE value -> oracle.json
  # OBSERVABLE = the RETURN VALUE of defaults(options, defaultsObj) — i.e. the mutated `options` object (same
  # reference when options was truthy; the fresh {} when options was falsy). Because the unit MUTATES the
  # input, the oracle MUST compare the RETURNED object (capture return), not a separate copy of the input.
  # The returned object is value-distinguishable: it reflects BOTH the pre-existing option values (kept) AND
  # the cloned defaults (added for absent/undefined keys). Grading is structural deep-equality on the result.
  # Throw paths (defaultsObj null/undefined) are recorded as {__throw: <message-or-class>}.

# =====================================================================================================
# SCOPE
# =====================================================================================================
# IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly): the full pipeline above —
#   (B0) options = options || {} falsy-guard producing a fresh {} for falsy options;
#   (B1) iterate Object.keys(defaultsObj) ONLY, in Object.keys order, leaving extra option keys untouched;
#   (B2) per-key fallback gated SOLELY on `typeof options[key] === 'undefined'` (absence or explicit
#        undefined), reading through the prototype chain, KEEPING all defined values incl. null/0/false/''/NaN;
#   (B3) mutate-in-place + return the same reference;
#   and the INLINED deep clone of each default value: primitive passthrough, array/object deep copy with the
#   proto-collision skip set, Date(getTime), RegExp(g/i/m + truthy lastIndex), Buffer(allocUnsafe+copy),
#   shared-ref/cycle preservation within one default value's subgraph (circular=true), depth=Infinity.
#
# CARRIED DATA: none. No literal tables; nothing to attest.
#
# OUT-OF-CONTRACT / OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (documented for the blind re-emitter; genInputs does NOT emit these for
# the differential because the oracle JSON-ish codec cannot round-trip them or they are degenerate):
#   - FUNCTION as a default VALUE: clone returns it BY REFERENCE (C3) — not codec-faithful; not emitted.
#     (Behavior: options[key] gets the SAME function reference; documented, re-derivable from spec.)
#   - SYMBOL as a value, or SYMBOL KEYS: clone@1.0.4's for-in loop copies only string keys (no symbol phase
#     in 1.0.4); symbols don't survive the JSON codec -> not emitted.
#   - `undefined` as a default VALUE: clone(undefined) returns undefined (C3); options[key] is then SET to
#     undefined (an own key with value undefined). This is IN-CONTRACT but `undefined` is not codec-faithful
#     as an object value -> not emitted; re-derivable from spec.
#   - Map / Set / Promise / Error / typed-array / boxed-primitive (new Number/String/Boolean) as a default
#     value: takes the C8 plain-object path -> a structurally-degenerate clone (e.g. broken pseudo-Set with
#     empty JSON, ArrayBuffer-less typed array). Not codec-stable / not meaningfully gradable -> not emitted.
#   - A default value object whose OWN key is literally `__proto__` (only constructible via JSON.parse or
#     Object.defineProperty): clone reassigns the clone's prototype instead of setting an own key (C10 note)
#     -> documented, not emitted by the generator (which uses safe object literals).
#   - defaultsObj being a PRIMITIVE: out-of-scope shape (string yields index keys, number yields []); not
#     emitted. The null/undefined defaultsObj THROW case IS emitted (codec-faithful, gradable as {__throw}).
#   - options being a NON-plain object (array, class instance, object with inherited props): in-contract
#     (array options ARE mutated by Object.keys(defaults) index assignment; inherited props suppress
#     defaults). The generator emits a curated array-options case and otherwise keeps options to plain own-
#     property literals so the inherited-prop suppression stays unambiguous.

# =====================================================================================================
# PROVENANCE
# =====================================================================================================
PROVENANCE defaults@1.0.4 (sindresorhus / git://github.com/sindresorhus/node-defaults.git, CJS), MIT.
  source: <node_modules>/defaults/index.js  (module.exports = the default-export function)
  inlined dep: clone@1.0.4 (pvorb/node-clone, MIT, clone.js — module.exports = clone). Called as clone(value)
               -> circular=true, depth=Infinity, prototype=undefined. Only the exercised subset of clone's
               type ladder (Array/RegExp/Date/Buffer/plain-object + proto-collision skip + circular tracking)
               is inlined; clone@1.0.4 has NO Map/Set/Promise/Error branches (those were added in clone@2.x).
  All cited behaviors VERIFIED by executing the real defaults+clone packages (Node v24.16.0, 2026-06-26):
  same-ref mutation, iteration set, undefined-only fallback (null/0/false/'' kept), deep clone of nested
  objects/arrays/Dates/RegExp, proto-collision skip-set, and the null/undefined-defaultsObj TypeError.
